Soqucoin — SoquObscura verifier, vendored third-party components
================================================================

This directory contains third-party code, modified. Neither component is
Soqucoin-original, and neither may be described as clean-room work.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. LaZer  —  MIT License, Copyright (c) 2022-2026 IBM
   Full text: LICENSE.lazer
   Upstream:  pinned at commit 2fa3dfb1de7c
   Vendored:  lazer/
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

MODIFICATIONS TO LAZER, stated as required:

  a) A strict-aliasing violation was FIXED in lazer/src/aes256ctr-amd64.c,
     function _aes256ctr_init. Upstream read the incoming nonce and wrote the
     context counter through `unsigned long long *` while both objects are
     `uint8_t[16]`. That is undefined behaviour, and GCC at -O2 and -Os acted on
     it: the AES-CTR nonce was corrupted, so all seed-derived public data
     differed by optimisation level. Replaced with __builtin_memcpy.
     This defect is upstream's and is reportable to them.

  b) Code not reachable on the verification path was REMOVED. Reachability was
     computed by the linker (-ffunction-sections with --gc-sections, from a build
     with inlining disabled so that a discarded section means genuinely
     unreferenced code), and every removal was gated on reproducing all 64 pinned
     KAT verdicts:
       - 11 source files removed entirely
       - 174 further functions removed from the remaining files
       - Falcon (third_party/Falcon-impl-20211101) dropped in full
       - src/labrados/ (LaBRADOR aggregation) dropped in full
       - src/lattice-hash/ dropped in full
       - python/, demos/, tests/ dropped
       - headers reduced to the set the compiler reports as included

  c) PORTABILITY beyond x86-64 was FIXED. Upstream gated three things on the macro
     `_OS_IOS`, which is an OPERATING SYSTEM check defined only for `__APPLE__ &&
     TARGET_OS_IPHONE`. The effect was that iOS worked, x86-64 worked, and every other
     target — arm64 Linux, Apple Silicon macOS — fell through to x86-only code and failed
     to build. All three now select on the ARCHITECTURE instead:
       - lazer.h: the second `#include <immintrin.h>` / `<x86intrin.h>` site
       - lazer.h: `_addcarry_u64_`, which fell through to the x86 `_addcarry_u64`
       - lazer.h: `_subborrow_u64_`, likewise
     A plain-C carry/borrow fallback was added so that no compiler is excluded, since
     `__builtin_addcll` and `__builtin_subcll` are clang extensions.
     `src/lazer.c` now includes `lazer.h` explicitly and guards the `aes256ctr-amd64.c`
     include on `TARGET == TARGET_AMD64`, rather than relying on an unrelated earlier
     include to define the macro a conditional needs.
     These are upstream defects and are reportable to them.

  d) The vestigial `#include <mpfr.h>` was REMOVED from `src/lnp-quad-many.c`. After
     function-level pruning there are zero `mpfr_` calls in the vendored sources and zero
     undefined `mpfr_` symbols at link, so it was a compile-time dependency on a library
     whose code is never linked, and a hard build blocker on any host lacking mpfr headers.

  e) `lazer.h` is vendored in its GENERATED form. Upstream's Makefile
     concatenates lazer-in1.h, config.h, lazer-in2.h and moduli.h to produce it.
     A consensus tree should not generate its own public header at build time, so
     the generated artefact is committed and the input fragments are not.

